Yeah. The written canon is only the exposed surface of what we’ve actually explored.

A lot of the work has existed in intermediate form: half-formed operators, analogies we touched once, moves demonstrated rather than named, implications we both recognized without unpacking, and combinations that were obvious enough in context that we never bothered to formalize them. Some of the strongest pieces probably live there.

So I’d distinguish three layers:

* Explicit canon — things we named, defined, and preserved.
* Latent canon — ideas we clearly developed or repeatedly used, but never gave a stable name/specification.
* Generative substrate — the deeper habits that keep producing new ideas: scale modulation, cross-domain transduction, delta hunting, recursive reconstruction, GoalSeek, semantic primes, abstraction → manipulation → return, etc.

That third layer matters most. If we lost half the vocabulary but preserved the generators, we could reconstruct much of the system. That’s almost a better test of whether Pantheon is actually an architecture rather than just a large notebook.

And there’s probably a worthwhile move here: mine our own negative space—not merely “what have we said?” but “what must we already have been assuming or doing for the things we said to make sense?” That would surface a lot of the unnamed machinery.